\title{Offline Solvency II Calculations and Regulatory Parameters}
\description{
Native R components for insurance solvency analysis, with explicit rule
profiles, regulatory parameters, synthetic reference data and auditable
results. Individual formulas can be used independently of the graph engine.
}
\section{Getting started}{

Create a \code{\link[=Context]{Context()}} to select valuation date, information date, currency and
rule profile. Call a granular function such as \code{\link[=intangible_risk]{intangible_risk()}} and inspect
its \code{value}, \code{unit}, \code{sources}, \code{details} and \code{status}. Monetary amounts use the
context currency, without an implicit thousands multiplier. Rates and ratios
are decimal fractions unless the individual help page states otherwise.
}

\section{Finding functions and data}{

\code{\link[=api_catalogue]{api_catalogue()}} lists the reference API and \code{\link[=coverage]{coverage()}} describes the
supported scope. \code{\link[=rule_profiles]{rule_profiles()}} exposes dated rule sets. \code{\link[=parameter]{parameter()}}
reads scalars, tables and correlation or catastrophe resources.
\code{\link[=reference_cases]{reference_cases()}}, \code{\link[=reference_case]{reference_case()}}, \code{\link[=reference_portfolios]{reference_portfolios()}} and
\code{\link[=reference_dataset]{reference_dataset()}} provide synthetic inputs and fixed expected outcomes.
\code{vignette("getting-started", package = "solvency2")} introduces the workflow;
\code{\link[=documentation]{documentation()}} provides additional installed, offline documentation.
}

\section{Main interfaces}{

Valuation and capital calculations include \code{\link[=best_estimate_cashflows]{best_estimate_cashflows()}},
\code{\link[=aggregate_risk]{aggregate_risk()}}, \code{\link[=basic_scr]{basic_scr()}}, \code{\link[=scr_standard_formula]{scr_standard_formula()}} and \code{\link[=mcr_combined]{mcr_combined()}}.
Use \code{\link[=Engine]{Engine()}} or \code{\link[=calculate]{calculate()}} for explicitly linked calculations.
\code{\link[=DiscountCurve]{DiscountCurve()}} and the local JSON/CSV adapters accept user-supplied curves.
\code{\link[=NumericOverride]{NumericOverride()}} records context-local sensitivities without changing
installed regulatory resources. \code{\link[=reporting_catalog]{reporting_catalog()}}, \code{\link[=reporting_data]{reporting_data()}},
\code{\link[=reporting_bound_rule_check]{reporting_bound_rule_check()}} and \code{\link[=reporting_instance]{reporting_instance()}} expose archived
definitions, component checks and draft XBRL generation.
}

\section{Scope and responsibility}{

Runtime is offline. The package does not obtain market observations, develop
insurer-specific internal models, infer approvals or submit filings.
Synthetic examples are not production market data. Profile validity and
documented qualification limits remain explicit; a successful calculation or
software test is not supervisory approval. Invalid or unsupported inputs
produce structured \code{\link[=CalculationError]{CalculationError()}} conditions. See the individual
function's assumptions and \code{documentation("LIMITATIONS")} before use.
}

